  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ All prime numbers are not divisible by $5.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $P$ be the set of prime numbers. The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all $x\\in P,$ $x$ is not divisible by $5.$\n\nIntroducing the universal quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\,$ $\u2200x\\in P,\\,x\\text{\u00a0is not divisible by\u00a0}5.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u00ac(\u2200x\\in P,\\,x\\text{\u00a0is not divisible by\u00a0}5.) & \u2261\u2203x\\in P,\\,\u00ac(x\\text{\u00a0is not divisible by\u00a0}5.) \\\\  & \u2261\u2203x\\in P,\\,x\\text{\u00a0is divisible by\u00a0}5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\,$ There exists $x\\in P$ such that $x$ is divisible by $5.$\n\nor simply\n\n$\\,$ Some prime numbers are divisible by $5.$",
